00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.100
Abraham: A cyber security incident
log is a short document created and

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.620
maintained by cyber security professionals
that indicates most of the relevant

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.890
information about a security incident.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.660
Nicole: Drawing information from
Cyber security log files can be

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.309
instrumental in mitigating attacks
and re-establishing a system.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.950
Abraham: The more up to date a log is the
easier it is for the cyber security team

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.070
to understand the risks and to be able
to act quickly to mitigate an attack.

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:31.330
It also provides audit trails
for tracking elements, processes

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:33.810
or users involved in an attack.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.730
Nicole: In large organisations there
is often a huge volume of different

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.670
events that need to be logged from
minor incidents such as compromised

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:47.340
credentials, for example lost ID cards,
to network applications and operating

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.580
systems and other infrastructure elements
that generate their own event logs.

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:56.320
This data can help teams to
profile typical networking and user

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.310
activities, therefore making it
easier to spot suspicious activity

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:04.429
that stands out and this can help
logs become an early warning system.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.220
Abraham: There can be serious
legal implications related to

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.750
not keeping timely incident logs,
such as large fines or lawsuits.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:18.680
GDPR and the ‘Network Information
Systems Regulations of 2018’ require

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.850
organisations to report any high-risk
breaches within 72 hours of discovery.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.629
Up to date incident logs can also help to
reassure stakeholders that an organisation

00:01:29.629 --> 00:01:33.810
works hard to maintain control of its
cyber security responsibilities to

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.750
prevent breaches and loss of data.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.710
Nicole: So, what other steps
should be included within a cyber

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.570
security incident response plan?

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.329
As we have already discussed
previously the incident response

00:01:45.349 --> 00:01:47.829
lifecycle is broken down into phases:

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.230
• Abraham: Preparation

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.460
• Detection and Analysis

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:53.120
• Containment

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.160
• Eradication

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.199
• and Recovery; and Post-Event Activity.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.960
Nicole: Whereas an incident
response plan is more like a set

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.840
of instructions to follow which

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.520
according to the NCSC should include:

00:02:07.029 --> 00:02:11.080
Abraham: Key Contacts- these may include
contacts from a range of different

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.880
areas including the IR team and others
such as the legal department, senior

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.260
management and HR, but as incidents
can happen anytime and often anywhere

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.660
within an organization, they should
always take into consideration

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.440
the availability of the contacts.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.260
Nicole: This should always be kept up
to date and take into consideration

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:34.320
holidays and out of hours contacts
and there should be at least one

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.180
conference number, which is always
available for urgent incident calls.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:43.270
Abraham: Escalation criteria and the
process for making critical decisions

00:02:43.469 --> 00:02:47.320
such as when to escalate the situation
to include key decision makers.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.600
Having a flowchart, checklist or
traffic light system, can help determine

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.970
the next steps to be taken, related
to priority and potential severity.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.960
Nicole: A Basic Flowchart or
process should cover the full

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.310
incident lifecycle including
preparation, detection and analysis,

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.000
response, recovery and follow up.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:12.949
Abraham: Basic guidance on legal and
regulatory requirements with details of

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:18.189
when to engage legal support or HR and
guidelines on how to capture evidence

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.279
and contact the regulatory authorities.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.490
Nicole: To further enhance a plan,
it should contain a checklist to help

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:28.560
teams to organise themselves at each
stage of the plan and can be used

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:33.409
easily in an emergency and could include
forms for documenting and tracking

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.939
incidents and post incident reviews.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.940
Abraham: It would also benefit by
providing guidance on responding

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:43.130
to various incidents and how
to remediate and recover.

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:46.560
So why is it important to have an IR plan?

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.200
Not having a plan would make it difficult
for security teams to know what to do

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:53.239
or how to respond to any incidents.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.900
This could cause a delay and in turn
lead to more significant damage.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.480
Nicole: Having an IR plan enables
teams at all levels to respond quickly,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:07.339
following clearly defined guidelines
to help safeguard any data and systems.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:11.880
It also offers solutions to
mitigating responses and guidelines on

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.240
collecting and analysing information
to help prevent future attacks.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:21.519
Abraham: And finally, it helps everyone
to identify their role in the process and

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.940
knowing when to and who to escalate the
incident to, which in turn can prevent

00:04:25.969 --> 00:04:28.210
unauthorised actions taking place.

